## Claim
Text: "Someone who couldn't afford health care under Barack Obama's Affordable Care Act gets penalized $10,000"
Claim author: Capitalism
Claim date: October 20, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210116144247/https://www.facebook.com/Capitalists/photos/a.226491315514/10164464788875515

## Initial Q&A
### What was the maximum annual penalty amount for an individual under the Affordable Care Act's individual shared responsibility provision for the tax year 2020?
Answer: The search result does not answer the question, as it pertains to California's **state-level** Individual Shared Responsibility Penalty rather than the **federal** Affordable Care Act's individual shared responsibility provision. Notably, the federal ACA individual mandate penalty was reduced to **$0** starting in tax year 2019 as a result of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, meaning there was effectively no federal penalty for 2020. Therefore, this search result contains no information about the federal ACA penalty for 2020.

Source URL: https://www.ftb.ca.gov/about-ftb/newsroom/tax-news/october-2020/individual-shared-responsibility-penalty-how-it-is-calculated.html

### How was the penalty for not having minimum essential health coverage calculated under the Affordable Care Act (e.g., as a percentage of income or a flat dollar amount)?
Answer: The penalty for not having minimum essential health coverage under the Affordable Care Act was calculated as a fixed dollar amount or a percentage of income, whichever was greater. Specifically, it was "assessed as 2.5% percent of family income in excess of tax filing thresholds" or a flat rate of "$695 per adult plus $347.50 per child, up to $2,085 for the family," with the total penalty also capped at the national average premium for a bronze plan.

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/how-many-of-the-uninsured-can-purchase-a-marketplace-plan-for-less-than-their-shared-responsibility-penalty/

### Did the Affordable Care Act include exemptions from the penalty for individuals who could not afford health insurance based on their income?
Answer: Yes, the Affordable Care Act included exemptions from the individual mandate penalty for individuals who could not afford health insurance based on their income. Specifically, people were exempt if they had to pay more than 8.13% of their household income for the lowest-cost available coverage, as cited: "people who have to pay more than 8.13% of household income for insurance... are exempt from the penalty."

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/

### What was the specific income threshold or affordability test used to qualify for an exemption from the ACA's penalty due to unaffordable coverage?
Answer: Based on the search result, the ACA's affordability threshold for determining whether coverage was unaffordable (and thus potentially qualifying for an exemption from the individual mandate penalty) was originally set at **9.5% of an employee's household income**. As the source states: "Originally set at 9.5 of an employee's household income, the IRS adjusts the premium affordability threshold annually for inflation." If the cost of the least expensive employer-sponsored plan that met minimum essential coverage requirements exceeded this percentage of household income, the coverage was deemed unaffordable. Additionally, the source describes a Federal Poverty Line safe harbor that "deems coverage affordable if the required monthly contribution does not exceed 9.5 percent (adjusted annually) of the federal poverty line (FPL) for a single individual." Employees with household income between 100% and 400% of the federal poverty level who lacked access to affordable employer-sponsored coverage were eligible for premium tax credits through the ACA marketplace exchange.

Source URL: https://www.shrm.org/topics-tools/news/benefits-compensation/affordable-care-act-coverage-terms

### Was the penalty under the Affordable Care Act ever set at a flat rate of $10,000 for an individual in any given year?
Answer: No, the penalty under the Affordable Care Act was never set at a flat rate of $10,000 for an individual. The penalty was calculated as a percentage of household income or a per-person flat amount (whichever was greater), and as the source notes, "The penalty could never exceed the national average cost for a Bronze plan." For context, average penalties were far lower: approximately $210 in 2014, $470 in 2015, and $667 in 2016. Even a middle-income family of four earning $60,000 faced a penalty of only $2,085 in 2018. The $10,000 figure cited in the claim has no basis in the actual structure of the ACA's individual mandate penalty.

Source URL: https://www.healthinsurance.org/glossary/individual-mandate/

### For a family, what was the maximum possible penalty under the Affordable Care Act's individual mandate for the tax year 2020?
Answer: For the tax year 2020, the maximum possible penalty under the Affordable Care Act's individual mandate was $0, as the shared-responsibility payment (SRP) "was reduced to $0 for months beginning after Dec. 31, 2018, by Section 11081 of the law known as the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act."

Source URL: https://www.journalofaccountancy.com/issues/2020/oct/individual-mandate-payment-a-priority-debt-in-bankruptcy.html

### When was the penalty for not having health insurance under the Affordable Care Act effectively eliminated at the federal level?
Answer: The penalty for not having health insurance under the Affordable Care Act was effectively eliminated at the federal level beginning in 2019, as the Tax Cut and Jobs Act of 2017 "reduced [the individual mandate penalty] to zero for years after 2018."

Source URL: https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties

### What was the legal basis for the Affordable Care Act's individual mandate penalty, and how was it characterized by the Supreme Court?
Answer: The Affordable Care Act's individual mandate penalty was upheld by the Supreme Court not under the Commerce Clause (which the Court held did not authorize compelling individuals to purchase insurance), but under Congress's **taxing power** under Article I, §8, cl. 1. Chief Justice Roberts concluded that the "[s]hared responsibility payment," though labeled a "penalty" in the statute, could "reasonably be characterized as a tax" for constitutional purposes because it was collected by the IRS, calculated based on income and filing status, produced revenue, lacked a scienter requirement, and was not so high as to leave no real choice but to buy insurance. The Court explicitly distinguished the constitutional characterization from the statutory label, noting that while Congress's decision to call it a "penalty" was "fatal to the application of the Anti-Injunction Act," it did "not control whether an exaction is within Congress's power to tax." The Court applied a functional approach, "[d]isregarding the designation of the exaction, and viewing its substance and application," to sustain the provision as a valid exercise of the taxing power.

Source URL: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/11-393

### Are there any documented cases or official IRS guidance stating that an individual was penalized $10,000 solely for not having ACA-compliant health insurance?
Answer: The search result does not document any case or official IRS guidance of an individual being penalized $10,000 solely for lacking ACA-compliant health insurance. Instead, it details the penalty structure, which for 2016 was "the higher of these two amounts: $695 per adult... up to a maximum $2,085 for a family, or 2.5 percent of family income," with average penalties for those ineligible for subsidies estimated at $1,450.

Source URL: https://kffhealthnews.org/news/uninsured-people-eligible-for-obamacare-face-average-969-penalty-in-2016/

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are that the Affordable Care Act's individual mandate penalty was calculated as a percentage of household income or a per-person flat amount, whichever was greater, and was capped at the national average cost of a Bronze plan. The penalty was never a flat $10,000 for an individual; for example, average penalties in 2016 were around $667, and even a family of four earning $60,000 faced a penalty of only $2,085 in 2018. Furthermore, the penalty was reduced to $0 starting in 2019 due to the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. The ACA also included exemptions for individuals who could not afford coverage, such as if the cost exceeded 8.13% of household income.

The evidence is clear and comprehensive, showing that the $10,000 penalty figure has no basis in the ACA's structure, and that affordability exemptions existed. No further investigation is needed.

The claim that someone who couldn't afford healthcare under the ACA gets penalized $10,000 is explicitly refuted by the evidence, which shows the penalty was never that high and included affordability exemptions. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The Affordable Care Act’s penalty for lacking health insurance was never a flat $10,000; it was calculated as a percentage of income or a per-person amount, whichever was greater, and was [capped at the average Bronze plan premium](https://www.healthinsurance.org/glossary/individual-mandate/). Average penalties were far lower (e.g., ~$667 in 2016), and the penalty was [reduced to $0 starting in 2019](https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties). The ACA also [exempted individuals if coverage cost exceeded 8.13% of household income](https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/).